      <h1 id="stage-12-glossary" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 12: Glossary<a href="#stage-12-glossary" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Add a glossary and link terms in the guide to their definitions. A <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> defines one term and can include abbreviations or synonyms. A <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> contains several entries in one file.</p>
      <p>Define a key for each entry. Use <code>&lt;term keyref&gt;</code> to link a term to its entry and <code>&lt;abbreviated-form keyref&gt;</code> to let the processor choose the expanded or abbreviated form.</p>
      <p><strong>Optional module.</strong> See <a href="/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/start-here/learning-path">Choose a learning path</a> for the starting checkpoint and the next core lesson.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 30 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 11 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded diagnostic examples below come from earlier runs. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. Run the gate for your current checkout.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-write-the-entries" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: Write the entries<a href="#step-1-write-the-entries" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>topics/glossary/</code></strong><strong> and the six entry files</strong> Each is a <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> with the glossary entry DOCTYPE: a <code>&lt;glossterm&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code>, nothing else.
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossentry id=\"gl-sample-rate\">\n  \u003cglossterm>Sample rate\u003c/glossterm>\n  \u003cglossdef>The number of audio samples captured per second, measured in hertz. Higher sample rates capture more detail. CD audio uses 44,100 Hz; video production typically uses 48,000 Hz.\u003c/glossdef>\n\u003c/glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossentry id=\"gl-bit-depth\">\n  \u003cglossterm>Bit depth\u003c/glossterm>\n  \u003cglossdef>The number of bits used to store each sample, which sets how precisely amplitude is recorded. 16-bit audio has 65,536 possible values; 24-bit has over 16 million.\u003c/glossdef>\n\u003c/glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossentry id=\"gl-clipping\">\n  \u003cglossterm>Clipping\u003c/glossterm>\n  \u003cglossdef>Distortion that occurs when a signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent. Clipped peaks are cut flat and cannot be repaired.\u003c/glossdef>\n\u003c/glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossentry id=\"gl-normalization\">\n  \u003cglossterm>Normalization\u003c/glossterm>\n  \u003cglossdef>Raising or lowering the level of a recording so that its loudest peak sits at a chosen target, giving consistent loudness across clips.\u003c/glossdef>\n\u003c/glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossentry id=\"gl-compression\">\n  \u003cglossterm>Compression\u003c/glossterm>\n  \u003cglossdef>Reducing the dynamic range of audio so that quiet parts are louder and loud parts quieter. Not to be confused with file compression such as MP3.\u003c/glossdef>\n\u003c/glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-compression.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossentry PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Entry//EN\" \"glossentry.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossentry id=\"gl-waveform\">\n  \u003cglossterm>Waveform\u003c/glossterm>\n  \u003cglossdef>The shape of a sound wave over time, drawn as amplitude against time. Audacity shows each track as a waveform.\u003c/glossdef>\n\u003c/glossentry>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>topics/glossary/audio-units.dita</code></strong> A <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> with two entries whose terms have abbreviations.
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE glossgroup PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Glossary Group//EN\" \"glossgroup.dtd\">\n\n\u003cglossgroup id=\"audio-units\">\n  \u003ctitle>Units\u003c/title>\n  \u003cglossentry id=\"gl-decibel\">\n    \u003cglossterm>decibel\u003c/glossterm>\n    \u003cglossdef>A logarithmic unit for the ratio between two levels. In audio editing, 0 dB is the loudest level a file can hold, and quieter sounds are negative.\u003c/glossdef>\n    \u003cglossBody>\n      \u003cglossSurfaceForm>decibel (dB)\u003c/glossSurfaceForm>\n      \u003cglossAlt>\n        \u003cglossAbbreviation>dB\u003c/glossAbbreviation>\n      \u003c/glossAlt>\n    \u003c/glossBody>\n  \u003c/glossentry>\n  \u003cglossentry id=\"gl-hertz\">\n    \u003cglossterm>hertz\u003c/glossterm>\n    \u003cglossdef>Cycles per second. Sample rates are given in hertz: 44,100 Hz means 44,100 samples every second.\u003c/glossdef>\n    \u003cglossBody>\n      \u003cglossSurfaceForm>hertz (Hz)\u003c/glossSurfaceForm>\n      \u003cglossAlt>\n        \u003cglossAbbreviation>Hz\u003c/glossAbbreviation>\n      \u003c/glossAlt>\n      \u003cglossAlt>\n        \u003cglossSynonym>cycles per second\u003c/glossSynonym>\n      \u003c/glossAlt>\n    \u003c/glossBody>\n  \u003c/glossentry>\n\u003c/glossgroup>"} lang="xml" title="topics/glossary/audio-units.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> is a topic: it has an <code>@id</code>, and <code>&lt;glossterm&gt;</code> takes the place of <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>. <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code> is the definition, a short paragraph.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> is also a topic, with its own DOCTYPE, <code>@id</code> and <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, containing <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> topics. In stage 13 the metadata policy treats it as a topic in its own right, so it gets a prolog like any other.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;glossBody&gt;</code> holds everything beyond the definition. <code>&lt;glossSurfaceForm&gt;</code> is how the term is written the first time a reader meets it, "decibel (dB)". Each <code>&lt;glossAlt&gt;</code> is one alternative form: a <code>&lt;glossAbbreviation&gt;</code> or a <code>&lt;glossSynonym&gt;</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-key-the-entries-and-put-them-in-the-guide" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: Key the entries and put them in the guide<a href="#step-2-key-the-entries-and-put-them-in-the-guide" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>keydefs-glossary.ditamap</code></strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n\u003cmap>\n  \u003ctitle>Glossary key definitions\u003c/title>\n\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-sample-rate\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-bit-depth\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-clipping\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-normalization\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-compression\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-waveform\" href=\"topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-decibel\" href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita#gl-decibel\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"gl-hertz\" href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita#gl-hertz\"/>\n\u003c/map>"} lang="xml" title="keydefs-glossary.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code></strong> The keydef map joins the key space by <code>&lt;mapref&gt;</code>, and a <em>Glossary</em> heading puts the group and the six entries in the table of contents.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n+++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@\n   \u003c/topicmeta>\n \n   \u003cmapref href=\"keydefs-product.ditamap\"/>\n+  \u003cmapref href=\"keydefs-glossary.ditamap\"/>\n   \u003ckeydef keys=\"start-here\" href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n   \u003c!-- Warehouses: pulled in by conref and conkeyref, never pages of their own. -->\n   \u003ckeydef keys=\"common-notes\" href=\"shared/common-notes.dita\"/>\n@@ -37,6 +38,16 @@\n       \u003c/topicmeta>\n     \u003c/topicref>\n   \u003c/topichead>\n+  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Glossary\">\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita\"/>\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-bit-depth.dita\"/>\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-clipping.dita\"/>\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\"/>\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\"/>\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-sample-rate.dita\"/>\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-waveform.dita\"/>\n+  \u003c/topichead>\n+\n   \u003creltable>\n     \u003ctitle>Concept, task and reference links\u003c/title>\n     \u003crelheader>"} lang="diff" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the keys</strong>
      <ul>
      <li>A key per entry, named <code>gl-…</code> so a glance at a <code>@keyref</code> says it is a glossary key.</li>
      <li>The two entries inside the group are keyed with a fragment, <code>audio-units.dita#gl-decibel</code>: the key resolves to the <code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> element by its <code>@id</code>, not to the group. That is what lets an <code>&lt;abbreviated-form&gt;</code> find the surface form of the right entry.</li>
      <li>The entries are ordinary topics in the map. DITA-OT publishes them as pages under <code>out/full/topics/glossary/</code>, and links from the text land on them.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-3-bind-the-terms-in-the-text" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: Bind the terms in the text<a href="#step-3-bind-the-terms-in-the-text" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit the three topics</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@\n     If you would rather start recording, go straight to \u003cxref href=\"recording-your-first-track.dita\"/> and come back later.\u003c/p>\n     \u003csection>\n       \u003ctitle>Waveforms\u003c/title>\n-      \u003cp>Sound travels through air as a continuous \u003cterm>waveform\u003c/term> of pressure changes.\n+      \u003cp>Sound travels through air as a continuous \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-waveform\">waveform\u003c/term> of pressure changes.\n       A microphone converts these pressure changes into an electrical signal.\n       To store the signal digitally, the computer takes thousands of measurements, called \u003cterm>samples\u003c/term>, of the signal's amplitude every second.\u003c/p>\n       \u003cfig id=\"fig-waveform\">\n@@ -34,7 +34,7 @@\n     \u003c/section>\n     \u003csection>\n       \u003ctitle>Sample rate\u003c/title>\n-      \u003cp>The \u003cterm>sample rate\u003c/term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in hertz (Hz).\n+      \u003cp>The \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-sample-rate\">sample rate\u003c/term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in \u003cabbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-hertz\"/>.\n       Common sample rates are:\u003c/p>\n       \u003csl>\n         \u003csli>44,100 Hz: CD quality, enough for most audio work\u003c/sli>\n@@ -45,7 +45,7 @@\n     \u003c/section>\n     \u003csection>\n       \u003ctitle>Bit depth\u003c/title>\n-      \u003cp>\u003cterm>Bit depth\u003c/term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.\u003c/p>\n+      \u003cp>\u003cterm keyref=\"gl-bit-depth\">Bit depth\u003c/term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.\u003c/p>\n       \u003csimpletable>\n         \u003csthead>\n           \u003cstentry>Bit depth\u003c/stentry>\n@@ -75,7 +75,7 @@\n         \u003cdlentry>\n           \u003cdt>Amplitude\u003c/dt>\n           \u003cdd>The height of a sound wave, corresponding to how loud the sound is.\n-          Measured in decibels (dB), a ratio against a reference level:\n+          Measured in \u003cabbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-decibel\"/>, a ratio against a reference level:\n           \u003cequation-block>\n             \u003cmathml>\n               \u003cm:math xmlns:m=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\">\n@@ -93,7 +93,9 @@\n           A level of \u003cequation-inline>\u003cmathml>\u003cm:math xmlns:m=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\u003cm:mrow>\u003cm:mo>−\u003c/m:mo>\u003cm:mn>6\u003c/m:mn>\u003cm:mtext>dB\u003c/m:mtext>\u003c/m:mrow>\u003c/m:math>\u003c/mathml>\u003cph>−6 dB\u003c/ph>\u003c/equation-inline> is half the amplitude of the reference.\u003c/dd>\n         \u003c/dlentry>\n         \u003cdlentry>\n-          \u003cdt>Clipping\u003c/dt>\n+          \u003cdt>\n+            \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-clipping\">Clipping\u003c/term>\n+          \u003c/dt>\n           \u003cdd>Distortion that occurs when the signal exceeds the maximum amplitude the system can represent.\n           It appears as flat-topped waveforms.\u003c/dd>\n         \u003c/dlentry>"} lang="diff" title="topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/preparing-to-record.dita\n+++ b/topics/preparing-to-record.dita\n@@ -10,9 +10,9 @@\n     \u003c/context>\n     \u003csteps-unordered>\n       \u003cstep>\n-        \u003ccmd>Speak at normal volume and watch the recording meter; aim for peaks around -12 dB.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd>Speak at normal volume and watch the recording meter; aim for peaks around -12 \u003cabbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-decibel\"/>.\u003c/cmd>\n         \u003ctutorialinfo>\n-          \u003cp>Leaving headroom avoids clipping, the flat-topped distortion that no effect can repair.\u003c/p>\n+          \u003cp>Leaving headroom avoids \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-clipping\">clipping\u003c/term>, the flat-topped distortion that no effect can repair.\u003c/p>\n         \u003c/tutorialinfo>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>"} lang="diff" title="topics/preparing-to-record.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n+++ b/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@\n   \u003crefbody>\n     \u003csection>\n       \u003cp>\u003ckeyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> imports and exports audio in several formats.\n-      \u003cterm>Lossless\u003c/term> formats keep every sample of the source; \u003cterm>lossy\u003c/term> formats discard detail to make smaller files.\n+      \u003cterm>Lossless\u003c/term> formats keep every sample at the recorded \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-bit-depth\">bit depth\u003c/term> of the source; \u003cterm>lossy\u003c/term> formats discard detail to make smaller files.\n       Exported files are named after the project, so \u003cfilepath>podcast-episode-1.aup3\u003c/filepath> exports as \u003cfilepath>podcast-episode-1.mp3\u003c/filepath>.\u003c/p>\n     \u003c/section>\n     \u003csection id=\"choosing\">\n@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@\n           \u003c/row>\n           \u003crow>\n             \u003centry>MP3\u003c/entry>\n-            \u003centry>Compressed\u003c/entry>\n+            \u003centry>\u003cterm keyref=\"gl-compression\">Compressed\u003c/term>\u003c/entry>\n             \u003centry>Lossy\u003c/entry>\n             \u003centry>Widely supported, small files.\n             Needs the \u003ci>LAME\u003c/i> encoder, bundled since version 2.3.2."} lang="diff" title="topics/supported-audio-formats.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the two forms</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;term keyref="gl-clipping"&gt;clipping&lt;/term&gt;</code> keeps the word as written and binds it to the entry. The output makes it a link to the definition, with the <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code> as the link's hover text. The content stays, so "Compressed" in a table cell and "clipping" in a sentence keep their own capitalization and grammar.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;abbreviated-form keyref="gl-decibel"/&gt;</code> is empty. The processor writes the term for you: the <code>&lt;glossSurfaceForm&gt;</code> where the reader first meets it, and the <code>&lt;glossAbbreviation&gt;</code> after that. In the HTML5 output of DITA-OT 4.3.5 each of the two topics that uses it shows <code>decibel (dB)</code>, linked to the entry, and "hertz (Hz)" likewise.</li>
      <li>The <code>&lt;term&gt;</code> elements without a <code>@keyref</code>, such as <em>Lossless</em> and <em>lossy</em>, are unchanged: they mark a term but have no entry to link to.</li>
      <li>The <em>Clipping</em> <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> is laid out on three lines because that is how the formatter writes a <code>&lt;dt&gt;</code> whose only content is an element; the meaning is unchanged.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-4-update-the-readme-and-run-the-gate" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 4: Update the README and run the gate<a href="#step-4-update-the-readme-and-run-the-gate" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Change the "You are on" line and the layout</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n \n Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n \n-You are on stage 11: reuse.\n+You are on stage 12: glossary.\n \n ## Stages\n "} lang="diff" title="README.md" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita topics/glossary/*.dita\nscripts/check-stage.sh"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"== validate-project  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n21 file(s): 21 valid, 0 invalid.\n\n== project-health  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\nRoot map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nProject is healthy: valid, no broken references, keys, orphans, or broken element ids.\n\n== build  project.json -> /tmp/check-stage-357688 ==\nall deliverables built\n\nSTAGE OK"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>Twenty-one files: nine topics, two shared topics, seven glossary files and three maps. Run <code>dita --project=project.json</code>, then open <code>out/full/topics/glossary/audio-units.html</code> after the build: the group is one page with both entries, each showing the term, the definition, the surface form and the alternative forms.</p>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <p>A glossary key is a key like any other. Change the <code>&lt;term keyref&gt;</code> in <em>Preparing to record</em> to <code>gl-clippng</code> and run the gate:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"== project-health  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\nRoot map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nUndefined keys (1):\n  /home/you/audacity-guide/topics/preparing-to-record.dita:15  key 'gl-clippng' not defined\nUnused keys (3):\n  start-here  [/home/you/audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:12]\n  digital-audio  [/home/you/audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:19]\n  gl-normalization  [/home/you/audacity-guide/keydefs-glossary.ditamap:10]\n\nSummary\n  Undefined keys                1\n  Unused keys                   3\nFAIL: project-health found issues"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p><code>gl-normalization</code> appears as unused because no topic mentions normalization yet; the entry is still published from the map. That line is information and does not fail the stage on its own.</p>
      <h2 id="what-you-learned" class="group scroll-mt-20">What you learned<a href="#what-you-learned" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;glossentry&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;glossterm&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;glossdef&gt;</code> is a topic per term; <code>&lt;glossgroup&gt;</code> is a topic that holds several.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;glossBody&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;glossSurfaceForm&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;glossAlt&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;glossAbbreviation&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;glossSynonym&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>A keydef map for the glossary, with keys to files and to entries inside a group by <code>file#id</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;term keyref&gt;</code> binds a written term to its entry; <code>&lt;abbreviated-form keyref&gt;</code> lets the processor write the surface form.</li>
      </ul>
